To solve this problem idiomatically in q, we should avoid iterating through all possible triples (which would be $O(n^3)$). Instead, we use the properties of modular arithmetic.

A sum of three numbers is a multiple of 3 if:
1. All three numbers have the same remainder when divided by 3.
2. All three numbers have different remainders when divided by 3 (one is $0 \pmod 3$, one is $1 \pmod 3$, and one is $2 \pmod 3$).

Here is the professional implementation:

```q
/ @overview 
/  Calculates the number of triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) where i < j < k
/  and a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is divisible by 3, where a[i] = i*i - i + 1.
get_max_triples:{[n]
    / Generate the sequence from 1 to n
    i: 1 + til n;

    / Calculate a[i] = i*i - i + 1
    / We only care about the value modulo 3
    a: (i*i - i + 1) mod 3;

    / Count occurrences of each remainder (0, 1, 2)
    / counts will be a list of 3 integers: [count of 0s, count of 1s, count of 2s]
    counts: count each 0 1 2 in a;

    c0: counts 0;
    c1: counts 1;
    c2: counts 2;

    / Combination formula nCr: n! / (r!(n-r)!)
    / For r=3: n*(n-1)*(n-2)/6
    comb3:{[n] n * (n-1) * (n-2) div 6};

    / Case 1: All three have the same remainder
    same: comb3[c0] + comb3[c1] + comb3[c2];

    / Case 2: All three have different remainders (one of each)
    diff: c0 * c1 * c2;

